   "subject": "Phylogenetically-corrected shape data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\">Hi all,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\">For my current work, I&#39;m attempting to implement a similar method to the phylogenetically-transformed measure of morphological disparity used a recent paper by Serb et al. (2017). Following the Adams (2014) method, I believe I have correctly phylogenetically-transformed my shape data, but I&#39;m hoping someone would be willing to confirm this for me. Is my understanding correct in that multiplication of the shape data by the phylogenetic transformation matrix (E) results in &quot;phylogenetically-corrected&quot; shape data to which one could apply standard metrics of disparity? (code below if needed). I don&#39;t want to do a regression, which is why I haven&#39;t used the geomorph morphol.disparity() function and have opted for this method instead.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\">My apologies for bothering you all with a relatively straight forward question, but it would ease my mind knowing I didn&#39;t make some grave calculation error. My phylogenetically transformed shape data are behaving somewhat strangely in my downstream analysis and wanted to make sure this wasn&#39;t the culprit. </div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\">Much appreciated,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\">Sarah</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\">Code for clarification:</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif\">y &lt;- shapedata</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif\">x &lt;- sizedata</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif\">C &lt;- vcv.phylo(tree)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif\">eigC &lt;- eigen(C)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif\">U &lt;- eigC$vectors</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif\">W &lt;- eigC$values</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif\">E &lt;- solve(U %*% diag(sqrt(W)) %*% t(U)) </div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\">y_cor &lt;- E %*% y</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\">x_cor &lt;- E %*% x</div><div style=\"outline:none;padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;width:22px;margin-top:2px\"><div role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Show trimmed content\" style=\"background-color:rgb(241,241,241);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-color:rgb(221,221,221);clear:both;line-height:6px;outline:none;width:20px\"><img src=\"https://ci6.googleusercontent.com/proxy/x7Az90uC0FlqG1NQNJvbQl7YtYRlnWzoqu2O_ZLb9xIKhYpJarh3aZrZ_qlU6_VKMQpRwI5_nDi9FsJOqf0XSQHw_6yv1VK7rhIBScGS2g=s0-d-e1-ft#https://ssl.gstatic.com/ui/v1/icons/mail/images/cleardot.gif\" style=\"background:url(&quot;&quot;) no-repeat;min-height:8px;opacity:0.3;width:20px\"></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
